y  getting the difference of the mass we  just took and the maps of the dried  lettuce and great this will allow us to  calculate the percent water of lettuce  and percent water of grapes and we will  then be able to compare the values that  we determine from this experiment with  reference values and published sources  now it's time to put the food into the  oven you should use an oven that is not  already preheated this is because if you  put the food into an oven it's already  preheated there is more likely to be  splattering which can change your  results so to decrease splattering put  the food into an oven that is cool that  way the slow heating up will prevent  spiring from economy so now it's in the  oven I'm going to set it to about 170  degrees Fahrenheit 170 degrees  Fahrenheit is about 77 degrees Celsius  we will let it bake for about an hour  remember this is when the water will  evaporate and eventually we will have  just the dried food in those little  aluminum foil pan will weigh those  subtract that mask from the mask when  the food was still wet and the  difference there will be the maps of  water lost then we can determine the  mass of the food that was water to the  percent of the mass of the food that was  why remember that we cut the great in  two halves and let them face up with the  flushing side up by increasing the  surface area we can increase the drying  time if you are interested in learning  more about the surface area to volume  ratio see my video on that subject now  let's look at our results after leaving  the lettuce of the grape in the oven at  that lower temperature for about an hour  to allow a partial dehydration I then  raised the temperature of the oven to  about 300 Fahrenheit just to allow the  dehydration to happen more quickly now  let's look at the numbers for lettuce  before dehydration so when the lettuce  still had all of its water content we  measured it at 40 grams after is at two  grams this means that the water content  of the original lettuce was 38 grams we  can now figure out what percentage of  the original lettuce mass was due to  water that is 38 / 40 which is equal to  about ninety five percent for the grapes  he found different results they were  originally 45 grams after dehydration  they were nine grams this means that the  water content of the original great  sample was 36 grams that means that the  percent water of the original grapes was  36 / 45 which is about eighty percent  water so we see here  at the great work about eighty percent  water the lettuce about ninety-five  percent water so with this experiment we  can see the water content of the various  foods that we eat it's easy enough to do  at home so I encourage you to try it you  can always google the percent water  value for whatever food you are working  with and see how close you got for  example I found reference values online  for grapes that agree with eighty  percent and reference values online for  lettuce that said about 95 or 96 percent  showing that we got pretty close so  that's it for today I hope you enjoyed  watching and we'll see you next time on  biology professor
 

